old_conclusion = r"""This paper presented a systematic mechanistic security evaluation of dynamic query selection in Real-Time Detection Transformers (RT-DETR). Benchmarked on the full Microsoft COCO val2017 dataset at canonical native resolution ($640 \times 640$, evaluating $S = 8,400$ candidate anchors), our $2 \times 2$ factorial feature-swap intervention demonstrated that while encoder memory corruption ($M$) represents the predominant factor ($\eta_p^2 = 0.902, p < 10^{-15}$), query prior eviction ($Q$) under AQSM exerts a major causal impact ($\eta_p^2 = 0.820$, $-29.12$ pp mAP drop under pure index replacement). Fine-grained telemetry revealed that proposal failure stems from selective eviction of localized anchors rather than total proposal starvation (Center-in-GT coverage remains $74.15\%$), and deformable attention weights do not scatter (normalized attention entropy remains invariant at $0.787 \to 0.783$ ($p = 0.828$)), suffering instead from \textit{Spatial Target Misrouting} (foreground hit rate plunging from $61.20\%$ to $35.80\%$). Controlled spatial perturbation experiments refuted the tautology hypothesis, proving that AQSM's degradation is driven by targeted center displacement rather than generic coordinate noise. Hardware profiling on a Raspberry Pi 5 and NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano confirmed that while RT-DETR is structurally immune to NMS computational sponge attacks, its query selection interface constitutes an unmitigated integrity vulnerability. Finally, evaluating an empirical runtime monitor against adaptive evasion proved that statistical heuristics can be bypassed, underscoring that intrinsic architectural hardening is indispensable for trustworthy real-time detection transformers."""

new_conclusion = r"""This paper presented a systematic mechanistic security evaluation of dynamic query selection in Real-Time Detection Transformers (RT-DETR). Benchmarked at canonical native resolution ($640 \times 640$, evaluating $S = 8,400$ candidate anchors across $N = 30$ stratified COCO val2017 scenes), our $2 \times 2$ factorial feature-swap intervention demonstrated that while encoder memory corruption ($M$) represents the leading factor ($\eta_p^2 = 0.902, p < 10^{-15}$), query prior eviction ($Q$) under AQSM exerts a substantial causal impact ($\eta_p^2 = 0.820$, $-29.12$ pp mAP drop under pure index replacement). Fine-grained telemetry revealed that proposal failure stems from selective eviction of localized foreground anchors ($98.44\%$ evicted) rather than total starvation (Center-in-GT coverage remains at $66.93\%$), and deformable attention weights do not scatter (normalized attention entropy remains invariant at $0.787 \to 0.783, p = 0.828$), suffering instead from \textit{Spatial Target Misrouting} (foreground hit rate collapsing from $65.54\%$ to $38.34\%$). Controlled spatial experiments demonstrated that AQSM's degradation is driven by targeted center displacement rather than generic coordinate noise. Hardware profiling on a Raspberry Pi 5 and NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano confirmed that while RT-DETR is structurally resilient to NMS computational sponge attacks, its query selection interface constitutes an unmitigated integrity vulnerability. Finally, evaluating an empirical runtime monitor against adaptive evasion showed that heuristic filtering is partially bypassed (TPR declining to $46.67\% - 53.33\%$), underscoring that intrinsic architectural hardening is indispensable for trustworthy real-time detection transformers."""

